To tell the Type System in TypeScript that a value may have multiple theoretical types but in practice will only have a single specific type, you can use a type assertion. A type assertion is a way to provide the compiler with additional information about the type of a value, bypassing the normal type inference process. Here's an example of how to do this:

```typescript
let value: string | number | boolean;
// Assign a value with multiple possible types
value = 1;

// Type assertion to ensure only one specific type
const singleTypeValue = (value as string).toLowerCase();
console.log(singleTypeValue); // "1"
```

In this example, `value` is initially declared as a union type of `string | number | boolean`. However, we assign a value of type `number` (1) to it. To make the compiler aware that we know the value will only be a `string`, we use a type assertion (`value as string`), which informs the type system that the value will be treated as a `string` from that point forward. The `.toLowerCase()` method is then applied to ensure `singleTypeValue` is of type `string`, as the compiler will no longer allow method calls on types that it doesn't think the value is now specified to be. This technique allows you to provide type system information that would not be inferred otherwise, making your code more expressive and easier to understand.

